A||||7||Patrick's Dad||Help with 3D Modeling....||||5||
Z||000000||Patrick's Dad||03-22-2001||10:59 AM||||Some of you have created Fieros (and other things) for NFS, so I though that you might be able to help.<P>I'm not sure, but this seems to be way beyond me. I seem to be somewhat computer literate, fairly talented (not expert) as an artist, and generally, cognitive enough of things that come my way that I can get an elemental grasp of how they function; i.e. an engine, large format photography and darkroom work, and even Adobe Photoshop....<P>So why can't I comprehend 3D modeling programs? I've had Ray Dreams (Uninstalled it. Got frustrated enough to curse at it.) and now have Inspire3D (Not too inspired by it....). Is it me? I'm trying to model fairly basic shapes (No human figures or Battlestars), but I just can't seem to get the program to do what I want it to do.<P>I'm trying to create a single model for my site (www.starhero.com), and maybe more stuff as it progresses (It's only two pages, and no Fieros, as yet.), but I can't see spending any more time with this. Is there a magic pill that will make me suddenly understand how I3D works? Is there a simpler program that I should be looking into? Should I just make a 2D think in PhotoShop?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/StarHeroSig.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||03-22-2001||04:52 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.digitalfusion.on.ca/portfolios/images/robert.jpg">  <P>I created this (first 3D character I've ever made.. also the last actually..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) with a program called Micrografx Simply 3D. It was done with primitives (simple shapes like spheres, donuts, cones) that were stretched or flattened to make the shapes I wanted. I've made a few other 3D objects with this program such as a Star Wars lightsaber (which I actually built, as a halloween prop..) and a retractable pen as a visual for a technical writing assignment last semester.<P>Anyway, start out with simple shapes first and you'll get the hang of it. I've focused more on 3D landscapes than 3D models, but I could probably get back into other 3D stuff if I wanted to devote the time to it. Another choice is to check on the net for 3D modelling tutorials, or buy a "3D modelling for dummies" book (or something similar)<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||loafer87gt||03-22-2001||05:46 PM||||I have been doing 3D modeling for about 6 years now. My advice is to your local book store or amazon for some training books on whatever program you are using. Another good resource is at <A HREF="http://www.3dcafe.com" TARGET=_blank>www.3dcafe.com</A>  They have a lot of good tutorials on their site as well as some good textures and models for download. Most 3D programs have a "wall" to overcome rather than a learning curve, but with patience you will find the time spent is well worth it. Here are a couple of my architectural renderings from a few years back. They were done using the original 3D Studio. I am now using 3DS Max version 3, Lightscape and CAD and the images you can produce is amazing.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PT.jpg">  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ER.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by loafer87gt (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||03-22-2001||08:57 PM||||LOL!! my stupid little character looks so lame compared to those, it's funny!!<P>very impressive!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||10
Z||000004||FieroJoe||03-22-2001||10:14 PM||||Yeah, those are really cool.<P>If you want to see some amazing landscapes and some posers (3D rendered figures/people) that have been 3D rendered mostly using Lightwave and Bryce, go to <A HREF="http://www.digitalblasphemy.com" TARGET=_blank>www.digitalblasphemy.com</A> <P>I always have one of the backgrounds there as my wallpaper, some really cool stuff!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||03-23-2001||12:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>Yeah, those are really cool.<P>If you want to see some amazing landscapes and some posers (3D rendered figures/people) that have been 3D rendered mostly using Lightwave and Bryce, go to <A HREF="http://www.digitalblasphemy.com" TARGET=_blank>www.digitalblasphemy.com</A> <P>I always have one of the backgrounds there as my wallpaper, some really cool stuff!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Or to get some cheap imitations, go to <A HREF="http://www.digitalfusion.on.ca" TARGET=_blank>http://www.digitalfusion.on.ca</A>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>(btw this is my business site...)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||6
Z||000006||loafer87gt||03-23-2001||12:33 AM||||Steve, check out <A HREF="http://www.3dnature.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.3dnature.com/</A>  and grab the demo for World Construction Kit. You'll love it. It even lets you animate grass and trees blowing in the wind, and will let you composite objects done in Lightwave or Max with your landscape. I used to have the program on my Amiga and was blown away with it. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||03-23-2001||02:08 AM||||I have that Micrographix Simply 3D and Picture publisher, both very good programs.  3D Studio Max is great, but expensive unless you find someone with it.  You can do some cool stuff with AutoCAD, but not as good and fast as Unigraphics.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
